  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest mesoanalysis, satellite and radar indicates the cap \nhas eroded across NW PA. ML CAPE ranges from 2000-3000 j/kg in\nthis region as well. Expect additional convective development\nalong a lake breeze boundary through the afternoon and evening,\nwith a southeastward movement. Additional convection could fire\nalong outflows as the cap continues to erode further southward\nthrough the afternoon and evening. Dry mid level air has\nresulted in DCAPE values around 1000 j/kg, which results in a\ndamaging wind potential for organized storms. A lesser threat of\nhail is also possible. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are again expected Saturday,\nespecially in the afternoon and evening, as diurnal\ndestabilization occurs. ML CAPE is progged from 1500-2500 j/kg\nby afternoon, with dry mid level air again resulting in a\ndamaging wind potential. Models also indicate more of a trigger\nwith a weak shortwave/surface boundary across the region. These\nscattered showers/thunderstorms should continue into Saturday\nnight, though the intensity should gradually decrease with\nwaning instability.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected to become likely through\nthe day on Sunday, continuing into Monday, with the approach of\na stronger shortwave trough. The severe weather potential is\nexpected to gradually shift sewd each day, with better\ninstability progged to the east. Sunday has the higher potential\nfor severe storms across the Upper Ohio Valley region, with\nbuilding diurnal instability (ML CAPE 1500-2500 j/kg.) There\nwill also be some potential for flooding, especially Sunday into\nMonday, though this will be highly dependent on location and\nextent of convection on prior days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHot and humid conditions will continue through Saturday. Issued\na Heat Advisory for a portion of the area, in coordination with\nsurrounding NWS offices, this evening through Saturday outside \nof the expanded extreme heat warning. Expect heat indices near \n100 in these areas. Further to the south, the combination of \ntemperatures in the lower to mid 90s and dew points in the lower\n70s will result in heat indices near 105 through Saturday \nafternoon. Decreasing 500 mb heights with the approach of the \nshortwave trough should result in lower temperatures and heat \nindices by Sunday, though conditions will still be warm and \nhumid.",
